ey_factors": ["Polling is thin but sits within a point of even: partisan pollsters showed Lombardo ahead by 3-12 over the summer, while nonpartisan Emerson had Ford up 2 in early September", "Lombardo's incumbency edge roughly cancels a midterm environment favoring Democrats", "All seven raters call it a Tossup (one Tilt R), and prediction markets price both sides between 47 and 52 percent", "Ford drew a seven-figure outside-spending boost in October, while affordability and gas prices dominate; Republicans attack his California fundraising ties"], "rationale": "Every signal points to a coin-flip: averages sit within about a point of even, raters unanimously call it a tossup, and markets price both candidates near 50 percent. Lombardo's incumbency advantage roughly offsets the Democrats' favorable midterm environment, leaving a slight Republican edge on fundamentals against a slight Democratic edge in the newest polling.", "watch": "A Lombardo-Ford debate, a late break on affordability or gas prices, or how independent Danielle Ford's vote splits could decide so close a race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Joe Lombardo (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nevada's governor race is a genuine tossup. Republican incumbent Joe Lombardo seeks a second term against Democratic Attorney General Aaron Ford, with independent Danielle Ford also on the ballot. All seven race raters call it a tossup, one tilting Republican, and prediction markets price the candidates nearly even, between 47 and 52 percent for each side.\n\nPolling is sparse and mixed. The only nonpartisan recent survey, an early-September Emerson poll, had Ford up two, while partisan pollsters through the summer showed Lombardo ahead by three to twelve points. Published averages sit within about a point of even. Ford drew a seven-figure outside-spending boost in early October, and affordability plus gas prices dominate the campaign's closing argument. Lombardo's incumbency edge roughly offsets a midt